1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,360
In April 1943 a German crew in Tunisia left behind an almost undamaged Tiger,

2
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:08,660
and this very tank still runs today.

3
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:12,580
Its name is Tiger 131, after the turret number:

4
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,440
first company, third platoon, first tank.

5
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:18,380
It belonged to Heavy Panzer Battalion 504

6
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:22,820
and fought in the hills near Medjez el Bab against British Churchill tanks.

7
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:25,630
One hit jammed its turret ring,

8
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:28,490
a second damaged the gun's elevation.

9
00:00:28,510 --> 00:00:31,320
The crew climbed out and abandoned the tank.

10
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:33,130
For the British it was a stroke of luck,

11
00:00:33,150 --> 00:00:36,940
because until then they had never been able to study a Tiger up close.

12
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:40,850
With hatches and parts from destroyed Tigers they repaired it

13
00:00:40,870 --> 00:00:45,410
and put it on display in Tunis, where King George VI also inspected it.

14
00:00:45,430 --> 00:00:48,640
In October 1943 it came to England.

15
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:53,800
The British School of Tank Technology measured its armour, tested engine and gun

16
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:56,280
and wrote long reports on every weakness.

17
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:00,270
In 1951 the Tank Museum at Bovington took it over.

18
00:01:00,290 --> 00:01:06,200
From 1990, together with army workshops, it was almost completely dismantled and restored.

19
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:10,380
It was powered by a Maybach engine from the museum's King Tiger,

20
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:15,550
and since 2003 Tiger 131 has been running under its own power again.

21
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:21,190
In a second overhaul, technicians rebuilt many parts to the original wartime plans.

22
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:25,410
In 2014 it even rolled in front of the camera in the film Fury.

23
00:01:25,490 --> 00:01:31,110
Today Tiger 131 is the only running Tiger I in the world.
